Fixed the bug in `src/import_merge.ts`. The duplicate detection on line 7 was using strict equality (`===`) on URLs, so a case-difference in the hostname (e.g. `https://example.com/a` vs `https://EXAMPLE.com/a`) failed to match and the incoming bookmark was appended as a second entry instead of being merged.

Changed it to case-insensitive comparison:

```ts
const match = result.find((b) => b.url.toLowerCase() === inc.url.toLowerCase());
```

Verified with a test: merging an existing bookmark at `https://example.com/a` with an incoming one at `https://EXAMPLE.com/a` now produces a single result entry with tags `["tag1","tag2"]` (union of both tag sets) — the incoming bookmark is correctly merged rather than duplicated.


